course: dictionary
lesson: 4228
title: What is grammar?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Grammar is the set of rules for a language: how words change and how they are put together into sentences. «The dog ate the bone» makes sense. «Dog the bone the ate» has the same words but breaks the rules, so it makes people laugh.
explain: Good grammar means using those rules correctly when you speak or write. Speakers follow grammar without even thinking about it, the way you walk without counting each step.
explain: In English lessons, grammar covers nouns, verbs, tenses, clauses and punctuation. Check your own sentences: does each word fit, and is it in the right order?
fact: Grammar means the rules of a language, about how words change and how they fit together into sentences.
fact: «Good grammar» means sentences that follow the rules correctly.
fact: Every language has grammar. British Sign Language (BSL) is a full language with its own grammar, different from English; it uses hand shapes, movements, the face and the body.
fact: In England, SATs grammar questions ask for Standard English, so pupils need the standard forms as well as their home forms.
fact: Spelling tip: «Grammar» ends in -ar, not -er.
exercise: «The dog ate the bone» makes sense, but «Dog the bone the ate» does not. What is the main difference? | Dogs cannot eat bones | It has a question mark | The second one has more letters | *The words are in a different order, which breaks the rules
exercise: Which ending is correct in the spelling of «grammar»? | -er | *-ar | -or | -ir
exercise: British Sign Language (BSL) is a full language with its own grammar. What does BSL use to show its grammar? | Numbers and symbols only | *Hand shapes, movements, the face and the body | Only spoken words, like English | Only written letters on paper
test: What does «good grammar» mean? | Sentences about food | Sentences that are very long | *Sentences that follow the rules correctly | Sentences written in capital letters
test: In England, SATs grammar questions ask for which kind of English? | Only slang | Only Scottish words | Only words from home | *Standard English
exam: Which is the best definition of grammar? | A school for very young children | A type of punctuation mark | *The rules of a language about how words change and are put together into sentences | A book of stories for young children
